Glencore earns $2.5 billion in stock sales
The Swiss-based mining giant Glencore scored $2.5 billion from the price of processed goods and sold its shares on Wednesday as a plan to distribute its debts.
Glencore, which is listed in London, has announced that it has been selling new shares worth $1.6 billion to pay off its own debt.
The company, which lost 57 per cent of its market value this year, was severely dissatisfied due to China's economic deceleration's increase over demand and caused turmoil in the markets.
Glencore's stock prices went up on the London stock market early Wednesday morning.
The FTSE increased by 2.77 per cent to 131.60 per cent in the FTSE 100 index.
Last week, Glencore unleashed a $2.5-billion-a-half-share stock sales plan last week, which was part of plans to mitigate the $30-billion-dollar debt.
The company has sold its new securities for 125 pennies per share, which was 2.4 per cent discounted according to Tuesday's closing.
Equivalent to 9.99 per cent of the group has sold 1.3 billion shares.
Concerns about China's long-standing growth slashed iron ore prices roughly halved, while charcoal, copper and other commodities fell between 20 and 40 per cent.
White House Verified Xi Jinping's Washington Visit
Chinese President Xi Jinping will pay an official visit to the United States at the end of the month.
Xi's first official excursion to Washington will take place on September 25th and was an extradition visit to Barack Obama, who came to Beijing last November, the statement said.
After the bilateral talks of the two heads of state, the President of the United States and First Lady Michelle Obama will pay a supper in honor of Xi and his wife Peng Liyuan.
"President Xi's visit will broaden US-China co--co-co-operation as well as relations between the two countries, as well as on regional and global issues, allowing President Obama and President Xi to handle disputed issues constructively," he stressed.
Xi, whose visit was announced for the first time in February, is expected to visit Seattle before D.C. to meet with some U.S. technology leaders and then move to New York City to attend the UN General Assembly in September.
Obama and Xi are also expected to address some controversial issues involving allegations that China is conducting cyber espionage activities, including Washington's allegations of domination in the South China Sea.
