'I was looking for two wrestling kids and I've got 26 people'
Mete Gümürhan's second documentary "Young Pehlivans," 66.
The Berlin Film Festival (February 11 - 21-21) was the first Turkish documentary to be selected for "Generation Lplus."
Gümürhan, who was originally from Edirne, but was a production company in the Netherlands, explained why and how he filmed the documentary, which he completed in the summer of 2014.
"Young Pehlivans" documentary, which tells the story of six Amasian boarding school students, which aims to be world champion in the field of wrestling, will compete in the "Generation Kplus" section of the Berlin Film Festival.
Young Pehlivans, the first Turkish-language to be selected in this section, are one of the nominees for the Crystal Bear award.
Although birth growth is Dutch, it is originally a Edirne director Mete Gümürhan.
When he was a child, he listened to oil wrestling stories from his grandfather and watched the competition in Turkey where he arrived during the summer holidays.
"There's not just political problems in Turkey."
I just wanted to tell the world what's going on in Anatolia.
"For example, wrestling from our horse's sports is unfortunately unknown," he wants to come together with six directors and prepare a documentary project.
This project is inconvenient, but one day the news of the magazine he reads on the plane opens the door of another project to Gümürhan.
'We were staying at the sugar factory'
The 10-11-year-year-old wrestler, Mete Gümürhan goes to Samsun where the family lives.
Their father won't let the film be filmed for the documentary.
Gümürhan explains: "I wondered what I could do all day.
"Efficiency from the Ministry of Culture could be withdrawn."
The father was talking about a boarding school in Amasya, where the kids were going.
I've been reminded to go there.
While I was looking for two kids, I ran into 26 kids who were wrestling at the school.
We've been preparing for a shot in a week and we've finished the permits.
Well, we didn't even have a place to stay.
"We stayed at the dormitory of the sugar factory."
The documentary, whose filming was completed in 3 weeks, was the biggest challenge to the director.
"I didn't know what I'd come up with when I'm shooting."
"They don't speak Turkish."
Young Pehlivans are focusing on the story of six out of 26 child wrestlers.
Baran, who was raised by his grandparents and had a weight problem, Harun who couldn't participate in the training because he broke his arm, Beytullah with ambitious but health problems, Ümit, the cute boy of the school, Ahmet the smallest champion, and Mohammad...
Mete Gümürhan describes his work with the children as follows: "I left them completely natural."
It's just that I've tried to use Mohammad all over the place.
I've been shooting at practice for the first and a half.
What I wanted was for the kids to pretend there weren't cameras.
"Unfortunately, they'll get used quickly."
It's a surprise to be elected to Berlin
Mete Gümürhan, who stated that he didn't expect Young Pehlivans to be elected to the Berlin Film Festival, said, "We had a preparation, but it was a surprise that we were elected to Berlin."
Young Pehlivans aren't a sports documentary, it's a production that tells the struggles of the children and transfers sections from their lives.
"I just wanted to bring to the forefront the stories of trying to find identities and coping with their problems for children with family problems," he says.
